course: dictionary
lesson: 3152
title: Stitching and stretching the truth
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Embroider has two meanings. To embroider cloth means to sew patterns or pictures onto it with coloured thread. To embroider a story means to add made-up details to make it more exciting. «Families wear embroidered shirts on Den Nezalezhnosti, Ukraine's Independence Day.» Here the shirts have patterns sewn on them, so embroidered means decorated with stitching.
explain: Use the context to choose the right meaning. If the sentence talks about cloth, thread, a needle or a shirt, it means sewing. If it talks about a story, or about adding things that did not happen, it means making things up. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Watch the phrases too. «Embroider a name» means sew the name onto something. «Embroider the truth» means add untrue parts, so the story becomes bigger than what really happened. The word is often used this way when someone is making a story more exciting.
fact: To embroider means to sew patterns or pictures onto cloth with coloured thread.
fact: To embroider a story means to add invented details to make it more exciting.
fact: A vyshyvanka is an embroidered shirt, worn on special days.
fact: Vyshyvanka Day is a Thursday in May, and people often wear their embroidered shirts then.
fact: Some vyshyvanka patterns have traditional meanings, but many are decoration and local style.
exercise: «Mia sat by the window to embroider a daisy on her cloth with red thread.» What does embroider mean here? | To paint over the cloth with glue | To wash the cloth in cold water | To tear the cloth into thin strips | *To sew a pattern onto cloth with coloured thread
exercise: «Tom did not catch a fish, but he began to embroider the story, adding a giant fish with golden fins.» What does embroider mean in this sentence? | To catch fish with a net | To sew a pattern onto cloth | *To add made-up details to make the story more exciting | To tell the story very slowly
exercise: «On Vyshyvanka Day, a Thursday in May, many people wear an embroidered shirt.» What is a vyshyvanka? | *An embroidered shirt, worn on special days | A birthday card | A blue-and-yellow flag | A Thursday in May
test: «Dad likes to embroider the truth when he tells his fishing stories.» What does this mean? | To make the story shorter | *To add untrue details to make the story more exciting | To tell the story exactly as it happened | To sew cloth with coloured thread
test: «Ella loved the embroidered shirt with its blue and yellow patterns.» What does embroidered mean here? | *Decorated with patterns sewn in coloured thread | Made from very thin paper | Torn at the sleeves | Covered in wet paint
exam: Which sentence uses embroider to mean sewing with coloured thread? | The reporter began to embroider the facts | Sam likes to embroider his stories with dragons | *Grandma will embroider my initials on the scarf | We will embroider the story together
